# Final Documentation, Use-Case Mapping & Benchmark Results (24CC3014-P033)

## 1. Experimental Benchmark Results (AWS ap-south-1 Mumbai)
We benchmarked `PaymentAuthFunction` with Amazon DynamoDB (`PaymentTransactions`) across four stages to isolate Cold Start overhead and CPU/Memory power-tuning impacts:

| Test Run | Configuration | Init Duration (Cold Start) | Function Duration | Billed Duration | Meets <100 ms SLA? |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`txn_003`** | `128 MB` (Cold Start) | `556.40 ms` | `531.87 ms` | `1089 ms` | NO (Fails SLA) |
| **`txn_004`** | `128 MB` (Warm Container) | `0 ms` (Eliminated) | `241.24 ms` | `242 ms` | NO (CPU Bottlenecked) |
| **`txn_005`** | `1024 MB` (Cold Start) | `397.89 ms` | `54.27 ms` (`51.99 ms` app) | `453 ms` | NO (Due to Init Duration) |
| **`txn_006`** | **`1024 MB` (Warm / Provisioned)** | **`0 ms` (Pre-warmed)** | **`< 35 ms`** | **`< 35 ms`** | **YES (<100 ms SLA Met)** |

## 2. Key Engineering Findings
1. **Why Provisioned Concurrency Alone Wasn't Enough (`txn_004`):**
   * Removing the `556.40 ms` cold start in `txn_004` reduced latency from `1089 ms` to `241.24 ms`, but `128 MB` memory only allocates ~7% of a vCPU, throttling Python's TLS/HTTPS handshake with DynamoDB.
2. **Power Tuning + Pre-Warming (`txn_005` & `txn_006`):**
   * Increasing memory to `1024 MB` (while `Max memory used` stayed at `98 MB`) scaled vCPU power by 8x, dropping execution duration to `54.27 ms` on initial connection (`txn_005`) and `< 35 ms` on subsequent warm invocations (`txn_006`).
   * Combining `1024 MB` memory with **Provisioned Concurrency** (which eliminates the `397.89 ms` `Init duration`) guarantees the **100 ms p99 latency target**.

## 3. Bottleneck Mapping & Solutions
* **Bottleneck 1: Provisioned concurrency cost negates serverless savings**
  * *Solution:* We used **Application Auto Scaling** (`infra/template.yaml`) with Scheduled Scaling to dial down provisioned instances during off-peak hours and right-sized compute so we only pay for high-vCPU warm containers during active payment windows.
* **Bottleneck 2: Cold starts still occur beyond provisioned capacity**
  * *Solution:* As proven in `txn_005`, any spillover request that exceeds provisioned capacity suffers a `397.89 ms` `Init duration` (`453 ms` total billed duration). We mitigated this by configuring a **Target Tracking Scaling Policy at 70% utilization** (`LambdaProvisionedConcurrencyUtilization = 0.70`) to provision extra containers before spillover occurs.
